right a new biography offers an  unprecedented look at the life of one of  America's most influential women Barbara  Bush is the only woman in US history to  advise both a husband and son during  their presidencies she remained one of  America's most admired women despite  being largely out of the public eye for  years before her death in 2018 for life  and role in American history are  chronicled in the matriarch Barbara Bush  and the making of an American dynasty  the book is written by USA Today  Washington bureau chief Susan page she  interviewed mrs. Bush five times in the  final six months of her life and is the  end is one of only two people outside  the family ever granted access to her  personal Diaries Susan good morning good  morning oh my gosh and it shows process  is so richly researched in so many new  details I know you talk to so many  people over 100 people right that's  right and you asked them if George Bush  hadn't been married to Barbara Pierce  would he have become president and what  was the conclusion you came to such a  generational divide people who were in  the bushes generation generally said yes  he would have become president  regardless but the younger you got the  more astonishing people thought the  question was because they were such  partners and the word that people kept  using was indispensable that they were  each indispensable to the other I ask  actually to the Bush's separately that  question and Barbara Bush said  immediately absolutely he was destined  to be to do all the things that he did  and I asked George Bush he thought about  it for a minute and he said yes I think  I would have become president surprised  that way but you know I think they are  both wrong about that because I think  this was a partnership the likes of  which we rarely see in life let's go  through because there's so many  interesting details you open up and one  of the opening chapters about the death  of Barbara's first daughter Robin who  died of leukemia at the age of 3 years  old why did you start the book with that  story you know I didn't originally in  the first draft of this book I had it  where it fell chronologically in the  first chapter was on the 1988 election  the election that put George HW Bush in  the White House and I realized that it  was all wrong that that was not the  defining event of her life  that the defining event of her life her  one of the most defining events was the  death of Robin and it shaped so many of  her views going forward it shaped for  one thing their own relationship because  she was the strong one  during Robins illness he was a strong  one in the aftermath of their illness  that was a pattern that they repeated  through decades that followed for  another thing even when you came to  things like her activism on AIDS that  related to what she saw the stigma  surrounding kids with leukemia who and  her daughter was suffering it was also  interesting to see the dynamic of  Barbara Bush and her mom how that mom  really how her mother really affected  her because you write that her mom was a  little mean to her and certainly seemed  to favor the other daughter who she  considered prettier and smarter than  Barbara Bush how that must have affected  her well her mother would often make  comments about her weight and Barbara  Bush was in fact sensitive about her  weight for the rest of her life and in  reading her Diaries often the first  thing she noticed about a woman she met  was whether she was slender or not  so clearly this was something on her  mind and you know it's a signature of  hers that she would make fun of herself  make fun of her make fun of her weight  and I think in part there was just a  germ of truth though that she that she  thought she wasn't pretty although she  was in fact a very handsome woman you  know when you think about that think  about her tough but yet she had so many  vulnerabilities her daughter Robin dies  in 1953 am i right sorry but then she  reveals that in the 1970s she suffered  through a great bout of depression where  she was suicidal did you talk to her  about that I did and this was something  that the only person she confided in was  her husband they come back from China he  had taken over being head of the CIA all  of a sudden she had been such an  integral part of his life and now he  could not share the CIA secrets with her  because as she said she couldn't keep a  secret  she was also going through menopause and  there may have been other factors as  well and she told me that she would find  herself so depressed that she wanted to  kill herself that she thought about  plowing her car into a tree or into the  path of an oncoming car and that she  would have to pull off the road and wait  that impulse to pass now after about six  months this depression lifted one thing  she did was go volunteer in a hospice  yeah  and that she thinks that was helpful in  her getting through this she says now it  was she told me that she felt  should have gone to a doctor she should  have gotten treatment there's treatment  for depression and that she would urge  other people to but it affected how she  felt about people who suffered from  depression because she didn't seem  judgmental about it because she  understood in her own life she  understood the point it had been let me  but how did she connect that did she  connect that with the role that women  were supposed to play in the generation  she came from and then in the emerging  one because there was a kind of Stoicism  you were supposed to maintain which  which undoubtedly must have made the  depression worse that's right and you  know one thing that happened during this  depression is to a small degree she  questioned the choices she had made in  life you know this was a time with the  rise of feminism women had all kinds of  options and opportunities that they  hadn't had when she was growing up and  she wondered if she had if her life had  real meaning if she had made a big  difference and one of her sons Neil told  me that he couldn't believe that she  felt that way that they talked about it  once he couldn't believe she felt that  way given all that she had done no it's  really it's it's juicy yeah it's it's  juicy and it's so smart and you really  give us some insight into who Barbara  Bush was I look at her very differently  now the things that we just really  didn't know about right you talk done  her views on abortion rights a woman  named Jennifer Fitzgerald who may have  had some sort of relationship with her  husband there's as you point out there  just so many things you can see Reagan  no American in American history - and to  see it through her life is yeah and even  the story behind wearing red there's a  story behind that and what does she want  the book to be called she didn't like  the matriarch she didn't like the matron  take the word dynasty I said at the end  of our first interview so what would you  call the book that the fat lady sings  again yeah I'm glad you went with the  major Barbara Bush in the making of  American dynasty Bravo well done really  congrats to you oh the name of the book  I'll tell you one more time you might  ever mention again the matriarch Barbara  Bush the making of American dynasty goes  on sale tomorrow wherever you like to  buy your books

